=== YAML_DIFF ===
Routing rule (per prompt 0041):
  - Per-tick driving fields  -> dest: drive_telemetry, column: <snake_case>.
  - also_signal_log: true    only when the field is referenced by name in
                             `migrations/000175_caggs_and_mvs.up.sql`
                             (the analytics-aggregate substitute for the
                             prompt's prose "consumed by an analytics
                             aggregate").

Aggregate cross-check vs `migrations/000175_caggs_and_mvs.up.sql`:
  cagg_battery_daily      filters by name on:
    BatteryLevel, ACChargingEnergyIn, DCChargingEnergyIn, EnergyRemaining
    -> NONE are in the driving category.
  cagg_climate_hourly     reads climate_snapshots columns directly
                          (no field-name FILTER).
  cagg_signal_hourly      groups by `field` generically over signal_log
                          (every signal in signal_log appears here, but
                          NOT a per-field consumer).
  cagg_fleet_stats        reads `drives` columns directly (drive-summary
                          fields, not per-tick atomics).
  cagg_vehicle_daily      reads positions columns directly.
  cagg_charging_summary   reads charging_sessions columns.
  mv_energy_daily         reads charging_sessions + drives columns.
  mv_position_hourly      reads positions columns.
  mv_signal_stats         groups by `field` generically over signal_log.

Conclusion: NONE of the 11 driving-category fields are referenced by
name in any analytics aggregate, so `also_signal_log: true` is set
on ZERO driving entries. The drive-summary writer rebuilds the
`drives` SI columns (energy_used_wh, regen_energy_wh, avg_speed_mps,
max_speed_mps, distance_m) from per-tick rows in drive_telemetry +
the latest signal_log values for SoC / odometer / position at drive
close-out time (per the comment in migrations/000172_drives_si.up.sql
lines 88-91); that close-out path does not require any driving-
category field to dual-write to signal_log.

Forward-looking column-name choices for drive_telemetry follow the
SI-canonical naming established in 000171_charging_si.up.sql
(`_w` Watts, `_wh` Watt-hours, `_v` Volts, `_a` Amperes, `_pct`
percent) extended to driving-specific quantities (`_mps` metres-
per-second, `_mps2` metres-per-second-squared). Boolean and
enumerated columns carry no unit suffix.

